166
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.840
So there's this past that I have

167
00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.910
that somewhat current of being an athlete.

168
00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:38.990
And that's true. I don't dismiss that.

169
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.560
I honor that. And I'm grateful when people recognize that,

170
00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:44.620
but at the same time,

171
00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.430
that's not the reality of how I'm living right now.

172
00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.010
And so, it lands kind of undeserved

173
00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:53.250
and the fact that it would make you uncomfortable

174
00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:54.730
is preposterous.

175
00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.677
And so, I would say to you in the same way like,

176
00:07:56.677 --> 00:07:57.510
"Oh my God,

177
00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.277
"this like incredible artist is coming here.

178
00:08:00.277 --> 00:08:02.846
"And he's like so full of life and creativity

179
00:08:02.846 --> 00:08:06.327
"and he's such an incredible storyteller and I'm nervous.

180
00:08:06.327 --> 00:08:08.094
"Like what am I gonna say to this guy?"

181
00:08:08.094 --> 00:08:11.400
Like how am I gonna help make this interesting

182
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:12.370
for other people?

183
00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:16.700
So, a lot of that is just being human.

184
00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:20.290
And I think it's also one thing that we share

185
00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.310
is just a history with addiction.

186
00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:23.940
I've been in recovery for a long time

187
00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.540
and I like struggle with just emotional sobriety

188
00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:28.550
every single day.

189
00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.723
And like trying to put a lid on my character defects

190
00:08:32.723 --> 00:08:37.530
and the negative kind of loops that play out in my head

191
00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:41.010
of unworthiness and undeservedness,

192
00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:44.650
and all of that kind of stuff that is maddening.

193
00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:46.140
I guess when I hear you say that,

194
00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.890
my version of that would be,

195
00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:51.320
I remember I went to an art school in Oakland.

196
00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.150
They changed the name but it used to be called

197
00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:55.110
California College of Arts and Crafts.

198
00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:56.920
And I was so nervous the first day.

199
00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.710
And I was like, this is where all the people

200
00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:02.530
that can really draw and my background was illustration

201
00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:03.850
and comics.

202
00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.535
So, I'd tweak out and just draw and draw.

203
00:09:05.535 --> 00:09:07.090
So I'm like,

204
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:09.190
I was known as the kid that can draw in my class.

205
00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:12.260
So I'm like, this is where that kid from every school

206
00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:13.093
is coming here.

207
00:09:13.093 --> 00:09:15.330
So, it's the test of the best.

208
00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.710
And I remember I had this illustration teacher, Vince Perez,

209
00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:21.270
and it was the first day of class

210
00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:22.860
and we got our first assignment.

211
00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:25.040
So, I didn't sleep for that whole week.

212
00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.870
And when I joined...

213
00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:32.210
When I presented my assignment, two kids,

214
00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.910
and I remember the names, I won't say, quit the class.

215
00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:36.929
'Cause they were like,

216
00:09:36.929 --> 00:09:39.293
if there's people doing stuff like this,

217
00:09:40.508 --> 00:09:42.336
I'll never live up to this.

218
00:09:42.336 --> 00:09:43.440
And they quit.

219
00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.980
And that gave me, at that time, so much joy.

220
00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.283
I was like, and then later in life,

221
00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.880
the way all my addictions manifested

222
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.090
with the one main addiction

223
00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.780
where you can hide in plain sight

224
00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.650
and no one will say anything.

225
00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.640
In fact, they'll pat you on the back

226
00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:03.230
and say good job is workaholism.

227
00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.160
So, about seven years ago

228
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:10.110
when I started to stand up, I was having art shows,

229
00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.843
selling paintings for millions of dollars,

230
00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.320
professional gambling.

231
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:17.160
I had my own podcast.

232
00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.590
I started doing the news for Vice

233
00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.943
and we won an Emmy that year that I joined.

234
00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.600
I was sleeping with multiple women in a day.

235
00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.210
I had the highest score on Angry Birds at the time.

236
00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:32.690
So, it was just like I had to...

237
00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:36.570
And I remember joining my first, like self-help group thing.

238
00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.917
And most of the guys were older than me

239
00:10:38.917 --> 00:10:40.930
and there's Titans of industry.

240
00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:44.350
And it was just I walked in with a less than attitude.

241
00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.320
And when I heard like, oh, I started this company,

242
00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.153
I did this.

243
00:10:47.153 --> 00:10:51.136
I have to show you like, oh, you're a workaholic,

244
00:10:51.136 --> 00:10:52.890
like I don't sleep.

245
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.110
I don't do drugs. I don't do this.

246
00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:56.410
And look at all these things like, whatever you did,

247
00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.500
I did a thousand times more.

248
00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.320
So, you're less than me. I'm more than you.

249
00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.320
And today I don't.

250
00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.383
Like that was such a reactive way to live life.

251
00:11:11.256 --> 00:11:12.760
It's like, you do this, then I do this.

252
00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.356
And it's like a game, it's like a war.

253
00:11:15.356 --> 00:11:16.720
It's all competition.

254
00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.470
I mean, I've heard you talk about how you from an early age,

255
00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.340
like approach your art from a competitive place.

256
00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.150
Like I don't do sports. This is my sport.

257
00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:27.810
How can I outwork everybody?

258
00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.900
And behind all of that, of course,

259
00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:34.280
is this like compulsion or drive to be seen,

260
00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:35.320
to be recognized

261
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:39.230
that derives out of the sense of abandonment

262
00:11:39.230 --> 00:11:42.770
from when you were younger and all the kind of like trauma

263
00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:47.200
that you experienced.

264
00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:52.200
And it is something that our society smiles upon,

265
00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:55.660
and with like comes to the creation of the ego, right?

266
00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:56.973
Which is the beast.

267
00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.140
As society is approving of you

268
00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:02.597
and you become more and more successful

269
00:12:02.597 --> 00:12:05.190
and your self-will gets attached to that,

270
00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.010
like I did this, I'm the one behind this.

271
00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:13.010
It becomes like, something that becomes really unmanageable.

272
00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.340
And it's not until it's too late,

273
00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.530
that you even are like in a place

274
00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:18.980
where you can reckon with it.

275
00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:29.170
Yeah. I tell myself all these stories.

276
00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:35.360
There's a story like I tell myself in my head

277
00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.710
of like who I am,

278
00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:38.610
and then there's stories I tell on podcasts

279
00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:39.500
or write in books.

280
00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.710
And then like that's a narrative

281
00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.450
that I've been telling myself for a long time.

282
00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.380
And is it true?

283
00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:48.350
Or is that just, is that my editor?

284
00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:49.770
You've told it so many times

285
00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:51.650
and you know it's gonna get a reaction.

286
00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:55.320
And your memory is really of the telling of the story

287
00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:56.687
and not of what actually happened.

288
00:12:56.687 --> 00:12:58.367
And my memory is horrible.

289
00:12:58.367 --> 00:12:59.358
(David and Rich laugh)

290
00:12:59.358 --> 00:13:01.420
My memory is very inaccurate and it's horrible.

291
00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:06.420
And I just remember being in so much pain,

292
00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:10.720
like it's both, right?

293
00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.480
I tend to be very binary.

294
00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.830
Life's awesome or life's the worst,

295
00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:15.050
but it was both.

296
00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:17.610
I remember having a very happy childhood

297
00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.850
at the same time being like just a lot of anxiety

298
00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.290
and pain all the time.

299
00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:28.290
And I got tired of people telling me

300
00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:30.080
I was such a good artist,

301
00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:33.150
like sometime in high school like when I was 13, 14, 15

302
00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:34.330
around that age,

303
00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:36.810
and I was growing up in Los Angeles,

304
00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:40.780
I was introduced to graffiti.

305
00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:42.880
But I didn't know this at the time,

306
00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.890
but graffiti has a lot of rules.

307
00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:46.010
There's a lot of structure.

308
00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:49.070
There's like this neighborhood you can't paint in.

309
00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.260
And these kinds of letters and I'm like,

310
00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:54.860
I don't wanna be a Vandal so I could follow more rules.

311
00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.120
Right. yeah, it's ironic.

312
00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.859
You know the band Sound Garden?

313
00:13:58.859 --> 00:13:59.692
Mmh.

314
00:13:59.692 --> 00:14:01.630
So in the trifecta of grunge right there,

315
00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:03.120
it's usually Nirvana, Pearl Jam,

316
00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.720
and then Southern Gardens third.

317
00:14:05.794 --> 00:14:08.590
And so, I used to listen to more, it's probably say Nirvana,

318
00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:13.192
but then there's this song, Slaves and Bulldozers,

319
00:14:13.192 --> 00:14:15.820
which is like a really long,

320
00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.240
it's like seven or nine-minute song.

321
00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.160
And it's, Chris Cornell's sings in like every single octave

322
00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:24.120
in that song.

323
00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.890
He's like the lowest view.

324
00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:26.733
And I would just...

325
00:14:26.733 --> 00:14:29.100
I remember being at my house and playing it

326
00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:31.910
as loud as possible.

327
00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:34.470
I had my headphones and I had it on a,

328
00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.410
I think I had it on cassette or CD

329
00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.700
and I would just listen to it over and over.

330
00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.770
And I would start getting myself hyped up

331
00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:44.270
for going out to do graffiti.

332
00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.210
And I wasn't part of a gang or crew or anything.

333
00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:49.310
It was just me and some spray paint that I stole.

334
00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.190
And I would listen to that song.

335
00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:54.437
And there's a lyric in the song where he goes,

336
00:14:54.437 --> 00:14:56.450
"Now I know why you've been shaking."

337
00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:59.070
And I didn't know, I was 15,

338
00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:00.360
I didn't know what that meant,

339
00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.840
but I loved that line and I would sing it back with him.

340
00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:06.570
And then I would start punching myself in the face

341
00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:09.590
soft at first and then like...

342
00:15:15.421 --> 00:15:16.840
Like I said, my background is drawing

343
00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.394
like drawings and like in a sketchbook.

344
00:15:19.394 --> 00:15:20.833
It's like this.

345
00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:24.700
It's my world.

346
00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:26.980
I draw in my sketch sketchbook and then I close it.

347
00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:31.310
The idea of going outside and painting something big

348
00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:33.200
that could be judged, that people would see me,

349
00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:34.680
it gave me a lot of anxiety.

350
00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:38.293
So, this is the way I would get myself hyped up.

351
00:15:38.293 --> 00:15:41.370
And then I would start like,

352
00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:45.580
what if another gang sees me drawing over their stuff?

353
00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:46.810
Or what if the police chase me?

354
00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:48.610
Or what if, like there's all these what ifs.

355
00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:51.570
So I would be like,

356
00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:55.860
there's nothing you can do to me that I can't do to myself.

357
00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.055
I'll hurt myself first. I'll get myself...

358
00:15:58.055 --> 00:16:01.820
So, I'd get myself hyped up.

359
00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:05.250
I already like get some adrenaline going

360
00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:06.490
just from like hitting myself

361
00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.430
and then I would go out.

362
00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:09.691
And then I get out there,

363
00:16:09.691 --> 00:16:14.691
I had no idea on how to paint using a spray can,

364
00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.820
like it's one thing to use a brush and a pencil.

365
00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:21.590
And so, I'd get out there and I'd freeze.

366
00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:23.379
I'd be like, I don't know what to do.

367
00:16:23.379 --> 00:16:24.212
(David laughs)

368
00:16:24.212 --> 00:16:27.340
And then I just started writing my name,

369
00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:28.506
David Choe.

370
00:16:28.506 --> 00:16:29.569
(David laughs)

371
00:16:29.569 --> 00:16:30.700
And it wasn't a tag.

372
00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:32.880
It wasn't like my alter ego

373
00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.250
and I would just start writing it.

374
00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:37.780
And I would draw like right next to it,

375
00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:39.710
I'd draw like a cute whale.

376
00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:41.830
And it was almost like prompting the police,

377
00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:43.400
like come get me.

378
00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.290
But I also knew...

379
00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.230
Like I got some unconscious level,

380
00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.090
like a cry for help in that early age.

381
00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:49.923
Exactly. Yeah.

382
00:16:49.923 --> 00:16:51.680
And that cry just gets louder and louder

383
00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.780
and louder as your life gets bigger.

384
00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:55.440
It got very loud. Yeah.

385
00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:56.520
It got very loud.

386
00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:01.520
And I was thinking about that recently

387
00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:07.060
with watching these people in Afghanistan

388
00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:10.890
like run onto the tarmac

389
00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.487
and jump on the airplanes while they're taking off

390
00:17:13.487 --> 00:17:15.087
and like falling to their death.

391
00:17:16.992 --> 00:17:17.825
And one of my character defects

392
00:17:17.825 --> 00:17:20.160
I've developed over my entire life

393
00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.663
is I have to be first in line.

394
00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:24.960
I know how to cut.

395
00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.910
I know how to lie, steal, manipulate.

396
00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:30.570
I know how to do anything that I need to do

397
00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:32.300
so that I don't have to wait in line.

398
00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.440
Like if we're at Magic Mountain, Disneyland, at the airport,

399
00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.760
I know what to say, I know every trick in the book.

400
00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:44.760
And like when I'm with my friends,

401
00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.400
when I'm with my wife, they say,

402
00:17:47.327 --> 00:17:48.807
"Why you got to do that, man?

403
00:17:48.807 --> 00:17:50.887
"Like do you gotta get somewhere?

404
00:17:50.887 --> 00:17:52.910
"Or are you in a hurry?"

405
00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:57.910
And I grew up with the stories of my family

406
00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:01.483
escaping from Korea, right?

407
00:18:01.483 --> 00:18:03.470
Like you're waiting in line to get on the boat,

408
00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:04.670
to get away from the Japanese.

409
00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:09.070
And so, I wasn't there, that never happened to me.

410
00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.360
That's a story from my grandparents, from my aunts,

411
00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.920
my uncles telling me these like horrendous stories.

412
00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:18.910
And so, I grow up with this imagined thing

413
00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:20.940
that may or may not happen one day, right?

414
00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:22.620
It's like one day,

415
00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.316
there's gonna be a tank that turns the corner

416
00:18:24.316 --> 00:18:26.750
and you better be the first on that boat.

417
00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:29.020
And when you get there to that line,

418
00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:31.820
you better claw, kick, scream.

419
00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:34.270
You better do anything. So...

420
00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:36.191
'Cause your life depends on it.

421
00:18:36.191 --> 00:18:37.580
It's almost like there's an epigenetic thing,

422
00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:40.330
like that's imprinted on your DNA and it gets passed down.

423
00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:42.500
So, even though you didn't suffer that trauma,

424
00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:43.710
it's like part of who you are,

425
00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:46.260
something gets triggered in you whenever you're in a line.

426
00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:48.720
But I think even on top of that,

427
00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:53.720
there's that like varnish or like veneer of the addict

428
00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:58.110
and the addicts entitlement,

429
00:18:58.110 --> 00:18:59.930
like don't you know who I think I am?

430
00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:01.710
Like I deserve to be at the front of the line.

431
00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:04.520
Is that part of that piece also?

432
00:19:04.520 --> 00:19:07.140
I feel so good right now talking to you,

433
00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.340
like listening to you talk 'cause it's like,

434
00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.040
something you said at the beginning of,

435
00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:17.683
like if I'm going on Howard Stern or like,

436
00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:21.160
I always feel like I have to over explain stuff

437
00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:23.154
or maybe talk over like,

438
00:19:23.154 --> 00:19:26.590
I'm in the world of recovery, which is not sexy.

439
00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.330
And I don't, for the most part, like talking about it,

440
00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:32.228
because I don't ever wanna explain to someone

441
00:19:32.228 --> 00:19:34.180
what a gambling addict is.

442
00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:35.752
Oh, that's a real addiction?

443
00:19:35.752 --> 00:19:40.752
So, I kind of overcompensate and go into like storytelling

444
00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:45.970
or like a cartoon character projection

445
00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:48.610
of what I think I am to people.

446
00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:52.376
And like, I feel good right now like talking to you

447
00:19:52.376 --> 00:19:53.209
and you're like...

448
00:19:53.209 --> 00:19:54.710
Well, it's that thing that you learn,

449
00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:58.340
like people that are really funny who are addicts,

450
00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:01.410
it becomes a defense mechanism or a coping mechanism.

451
00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:02.930
They can hide behind the joke.

452
00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:05.870
You know that you're an unbelievable storyteller.

453
00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:08.030
So, you can always pull out that card

454
00:20:08.030 --> 00:20:10.740
when you need to and get the approval.

455
00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:12.040
Or if you're Steve-O,

456
00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:14.420
you can do some crazy stunt

457
00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:16.100
and get the attention that you need.

458
00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:19.152
But it's really ultimately a deflection

459
00:20:19.152 --> 00:20:22.760
because you're protecting that like inner child

460
00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:27.760
that is wounded or afraid of speaking their truth.

461
00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:31.160
Yeah. And since you bring up Steve-O,

462
00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:34.670
I had dinner with him about two weeks ago,

463
00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:37.700
and he was on my show.

464
00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:41.130
And I know him casually. I've met with him like...

465
00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:43.520
He's a friend of mine. Yeah.

466
00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:45.680
So, I don't know him too well,

467
00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.650
so having dinner without a camera

468
00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:49.640
and it was nice to get to know him.

469
00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.410
And I don't think he would mind me saying this,

470
00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:55.510
but even though he's not a dad, he's got a lot of dad.

471
00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:58.940
He's like a dad, like, sorry I'm like burping.

472
00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:02.270
He's very concerned about like climate change

473
00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:04.070
and like the stimulus package.

474
00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:07.120
And he's just like concerned and I'm sitting there going,

475
00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.620
oh, like he's making all these good points.

476
00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:12.450
And I'm like, "Wait, what do you care?"

477
00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:15.450
Like you jump in a shark tank, like any day,

478
00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:17.560
like every single stunt you know you could die.

479
00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:21.973
And you're caring about like what the future of the world,

480
00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.017
And he said,

481
00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:30.017
"Hey, I don't think I was ever an adrenaline junkie."

482
00:21:31.568 --> 00:21:33.900
He's like the way his addictions manifested itself.

483
00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:37.470
And he would tell you, he's an attention-holic, right?

484
00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:39.313
He's like, all of it is so,

485
00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:46.100
and I go, "Would you ever do a stunt or something

486
00:21:48.137 --> 00:21:49.757
"and not broadcast it for TV?

487
00:21:49.757 --> 00:21:52.647
"Or like would you do it just just for yourself,

488
00:21:52.647 --> 00:21:54.610
"just to be like, I would."

489
00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:56.500
And I had actually had this conversation

490
00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:57.553
with Rainn Wilson also.

491
00:21:57.553 --> 00:22:02.553
He's like, everything I've ever done that's performative,

492
00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:07.231
it is a performance, it's for someone else.

493
00:22:07.231 --> 00:22:10.190
It's almost like, if it's not filmed,

494
00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:12.240
then it's like it never happened.

495
00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:14.760
And that's something that I really struggled with

496
00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:19.760
because my final art show was in Beverly Hills,

497
00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:24.270
on Rodale in 2009.

498
00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:28.670
And I was so checked out at that time,

499
00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:33.450
and it was my gallerist, Steve,

500
00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:37.173
he's same guy that represented Banksy.

501
00:22:38.882 --> 00:22:40.640
And he's just like, he's from London.

502
00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:42.250
He was like, "Where do you wanna do your next show, Dave?"

503
00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:44.137
And I was like, "It has to be in LA.

504
00:22:44.137 --> 00:22:46.237
"It has to be on the most expensive,

505
00:22:46.237 --> 00:22:48.727
"the most like right in Rodale Drive

506
00:22:48.727 --> 00:22:52.207
"and lights, camera, action.

507
00:22:52.207 --> 00:22:54.020
"I want every celebrity there."

508
00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:58.573
And it was just all like I had to show everyone,

509
00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:02.300
like I was enough and I was, I wasn't even enough.

510
00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:03.400
I was better than you.

511
00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.601
How did that workout?

512
00:23:07.601 --> 00:23:09.310
(David and Rich laugh)

513
00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:10.290
I just sat there.

514
00:23:10.290 --> 00:23:14.640
And they're telling me how much...

515
00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.300
You just sold out your show and the whole thing.

516
00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:18.593
And by that point,

517
00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:22.900
my name was starting to be in conversations

518
00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:27.640
with like Damien Hirst and Merck Cami, and all these.

519
00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:30.620
And I got to meet some of these people.

520
00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:33.960
And I was like, they're nice guys, whatever,

521
00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.860
but I'm like, "I don't ever wanna be these people."

522
00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:37.950
I don't wanna like...

523
00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:41.310
Usually when you get to that stage and you have a factory,

524
00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.960
and then you have other people painting your stuff

525
00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:45.344
and then you can sell stuff,

526
00:23:45.344 --> 00:23:47.740
it's never enough.

527
00:23:47.740 --> 00:23:49.920
It's just more and more and more.

528
00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:54.920
And I sat there and I just kind of said to myself,

529
00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:00.650
I don't wanna sell my art anymore.

530
00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:03.773
Like I don't...

531
00:24:03.773 --> 00:24:06.270
And it goes against everything of how I was raised

532
00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.850
and like survival and getting to the front of the line.

533
00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:12.010
I just said, it's enough.

534
00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:13.860
I've literally made enough money in my life.

535
00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:15.880
I don't ever have to work again,

536
00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:20.880
which is, every time I've said that out loud,

537
00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:24.303
it's abstract.

538
00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:29.170
It's abstract to say, I'll go one step further.

539
00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:31.314
I never have to move again.

540
00:24:31.314 --> 00:24:33.720
Like I never have to move again.

541
00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:37.040
Like I can hire someone to just push me around

542
00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.210
like the total addict side of me

543
00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:42.300
that wants to like have zero responsibilities

544
00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:43.550
and accountability.

545
00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:46.660
I can literally buy a humongous house

546
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:48.600
and have just someone wheel me around

547
00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.230
and just all the food and video games.

548
00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:54.100
And all my human desires and needs

549
00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:55.670
can just come right to my door

550
00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:57.260
and I don't have to do anything again.

551
00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:58.940
And if I start thinking about that,

552
00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:02.798
it messes me up 'cause that's not the life I want.

553
00:25:02.798 --> 00:25:07.480
Right. But you have an experiential adventure

554
00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:10.900
with all of that to arrive at that place.

555
00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:11.900
Like you had to dip your toe

556
00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:16.360
in every conceivable indulgence.

557
00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:19.250
But that's the thing is, as a human, as a man,

558
00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:23.770
like I knew I had people in my life that knew better.

559
00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.940
And they were like, they told me,

560
00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:28.367
they're like, "The writing's on the wall,

561
00:25:28.367 --> 00:25:30.950
"like this doesn't end well for you."

562
00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:33.377
And yet I had to be like, but let me see for myself.

563
00:25:33.377 --> 00:25:36.083
And the ego goes, well, it didn't work out for you.

564
00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:40.010
And even though you're smarter, more powerful,

565
00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:41.680
more better looking, more everything than me,

566
00:25:41.680 --> 00:25:43.040
like that's how it worked out for you.

567
00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:44.810
For me, it will be different.

568
00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:47.220
And to have more and more and more people tell me,

569
00:25:47.220 --> 00:25:48.860
I still had to see for myself.

570
00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:51.660
And I'm not that way today.

571
00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.180
If someone's like, "Hey, that's not a good idea."

572
00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:54.650
I go, "Okay. Over it."

573
00:25:54.650 --> 00:25:59.650
So, I pulled the plug on the commerce side

574
00:26:00.430 --> 00:26:05.130
of my art business 'cause I went back to...

575
00:26:08.002 --> 00:26:12.240
there's so much when you tell someone,

576
00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:15.040
or when I tell myself I wanna be an artist, right?

577
00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:17.590
It's not like I just sit down and I start painting.

578
00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:21.880
What is this for? Is this for a gallery?

579
00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.802
Is this for children's storybook?

580
00:26:24.802 --> 00:26:28.110
How much am I gonna sell it for?

581
00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:31.230
Like there's so much stuff that comes with it.

582
00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:32.890
And when I paint with kids,

583
00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:36.620
I do a lot of murals with like at-risk youth and stuff.

584
00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:37.860
There's none of that, right?

585
00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.330
As adults, we need a lot of explanation

586
00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:42.160
and we need a lot of handholding.

587
00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:45.600
We need a lot of what is this for?

588
00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.850
And there's so many questions.

589
00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:51.284
And then like with kids, when I say I teach them,

590
00:26:51.284 --> 00:26:54.170
I just put the tools.

591
00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:55.003
You giving them permission.

592
00:26:55.003 --> 00:26:57.030
Yeah, I just put the tools out and then they just start.

593
00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:57.863
I don't even...

594
00:26:57.863 --> 00:26:59.180
Expression in its purest form.

595
00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:02.479
So, stepping outside of the commerce piece

596
00:27:02.479 --> 00:27:04.270
provides that opportunity

597
00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:06.660
for you to reframe your relationship with your art,

598
00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:10.350
where it can be just about pure creative expression.

599
00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:12.670
And you being motivated

600
00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:17.040
only by this sense of having something you wanna say

601
00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.180
and saying it for no other reason.

602
00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:20.500
Right. Which is, it's hard.

603
00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:22.260
Which is the best.

604
00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:24.804
If you remove all those other motivations,

605
00:27:24.804 --> 00:27:27.200
isn't that gonna produce the best art anyway,

606
00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:28.970
or the purest art?

607
00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:31.230
But you said the word permission, which is,

608
00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:35.530
I think is huge because there was an art barn

609
00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:38.840
at the first rehab I ever went to.

610
00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:42.130
And for me, they're like, this is your lifestyle.

611
00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:43.530
I was actually banned from the art barn.

612
00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.130
You're the only guy who can't do that.

613
00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:47.660
They're like, this is your escape.

614
00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:50.280
So you're not alone. Like you gotta go do something else.

615
00:27:50.280 --> 00:27:52.080
But I remember peeking my head in there

616
00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:55.170
and it was a really expensive rehab.

617
00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:57.210
So, everyone in there is like a multimillionaire

618
00:27:57.210 --> 00:27:58.123
or a billionaire.

619
00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:01.255
There was a guy in there

620
00:28:01.255 --> 00:28:04.050
you could argue is one of the most powerful people

621
00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:05.210
on the planet.

622
00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:07.110
And he was asking permission

623
00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.260
if he could use like the glitter.

624
00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:10.440
They were all using normal paint,

625
00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:12.757
but then there was like a glitter section and he's like,

626
00:28:12.757 --> 00:28:13.860
"Can I use the glitter?"

627
00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:15.287
And I was like,

628
00:28:15.287 --> 00:28:17.007
"Motherfucker, you can buy this place.

629
00:28:17.007 --> 00:28:19.300
"You can buy this place. You can build your own."

630
00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:21.310
Like, and I go,

631
00:28:21.310 --> 00:28:26.310
that guy had to ask permission and then I just go back,

632
00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:31.160
'cause for kids, like young kids, they just do life, right?

633
00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:32.480
They just live life.

634
00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:37.253
And I think about how many things in my life,

635
00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:39.570
is this okay?

636
00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:40.783
Yeah.

637
00:28:40.783 --> 00:28:42.890
Like there's so much, oh, I'm sorry.

638
00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:44.107
Am I allowed to...

639
00:28:44.107 --> 00:28:49.107
And for me, it's 'cause, I was talking to Pako,

640
00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:55.440
the filmmaker who did the "Choe Show" with me.

641
00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:58.680
He literally flew in from the Philippines yesterday,

642
00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.117
he's crushed out at my house right now.

643
00:29:00.117 --> 00:29:02.410
And I talked to him at dinner last night

644
00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:07.410
about pugs, and those like little cardboard,

645
00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:12.930
things that you, I never played it

646
00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:14.270
but I guess you played it.

647
00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:16.210
And then you win someone else's pugs.

648
00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:21.210
So, his father, he worked at Pepsi in the Philippines

649
00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:27.180
and Pako was playing with Coca-Cola pugs.

650
00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:31.890
And it was a skill that he had to win other kids pugs.

651
00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.653
And at the end of the day, he had a stack,

652
00:29:34.653 --> 00:29:38.163
and he was so proud of this thing that he had done.

653
00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:42.170
And his dad comes home and he sees the Coca-Cola logo.

654
00:29:43.713 --> 00:29:44.546
And then he said like,

655
00:29:44.546 --> 00:29:46.210
like a movie had just started raining in Manila

656
00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:48.100
and it started turning into a typhoon

657
00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:50.357
and his dad took all his pugs and he's like,

658
00:29:50.357 --> 00:29:52.540
"Hey, dad, what are you doing? Like those are mine."

659
00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:55.070
And he threw him into the typhoon.

660
00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:57.263
And this is something that happened at his,

661
00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:00.030
I don't know how old he was, seven, eight?

662
00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:05.030
And so, and my version of that is in Korea town,

663
00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:11.830
my dad's friend, this is in the 80s, early 80s

664
00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:13.733
when Korea town was much smaller,

665
00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:18.560
his friend had opened a seafood restaurant.

666
00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.070
And like the way things worked with Korean immigrants

667
00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:25.810
is without getting too into it,

668
00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:27.410
it's like they would like loan each other money

669
00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:28.350
to start businesses.

670
00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:29.183
It was like support.

671
00:30:29.183 --> 00:30:31.580
Like you start your business then we come support you.

672
00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:35.127
And so, I remember dad in the car there going like,

673
00:30:35.127 --> 00:30:36.230
"Let me order."

674
00:30:36.230 --> 00:30:37.560
'Cause we didn't eat out that often.

675
00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:38.920
'Cause we didn't have that much money.

676
00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:40.223
And I know he was gonna get

677
00:30:40.223 --> 00:30:42.387
like the friends and family discount, but he was like,

678
00:30:42.387 --> 00:30:45.507
"Let me order because seafood can be expensive."

679
00:30:47.375 --> 00:30:51.640
And I remember going okay, cool.

680
00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:54.910
And we went there and I think it was my first time

681
00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:58.270
like at a real restaurant and I must have been six or seven,

682
00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:00.910
not like McDonald's or something.

683
00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:02.180
And I remember his friend,

684
00:31:02.180 --> 00:31:04.297
he was charming and he came with his thing and he's like,

685
00:31:04.297 --> 00:31:06.247
"And you little man, what do you want?"

686
00:31:07.659 --> 00:31:08.530
And there was a lobster tank

687
00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:10.570
and he's like the lobsters fresh today.

688
00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:14.690
Like I never had an adult asking me what I want.

689
00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:16.910
So, I'm like, "That, I'll have that."

690
00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:21.080
And I could just feel my dad glaring down at me,

691
00:31:21.080 --> 00:31:24.820
just like, and so then he took everyone's order.

692
00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.177
And then as soon as he left, my dad's like,

693
00:31:27.177 --> 00:31:30.750
"Do you know how much your fresh lobster costs?"

694
00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:32.893
And then my brothers jumped in,

695
00:31:32.893 --> 00:31:35.087
they're like, "Stupid David,

696
00:31:35.087 --> 00:31:37.500
"like dad said he's gonna order."

697
00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:38.530
Now we...

698
00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:42.447
So, I'm like, "You want me to get him?

699
00:31:42.447 --> 00:31:43.660
"Like should we change the..."

700
00:31:43.660 --> 00:31:46.580
He's like, "No. Then we're gonna sound like we're poor."

701
00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:49.810
And so, the shame.

702
00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:52.968
I'm telling you a story about a lobster meal,

703
00:31:52.968 --> 00:31:56.967
So, then the lobster comes, I can't even enjoy it, right?

704
00:31:56.967 --> 00:31:58.167
"Does it taste good?"

705
00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:00.507
I'm like, "Guys, I don't want it now.

706
00:32:00.507 --> 00:32:01.930
"Like you guys are been yelling at me."

707
00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:03.777
He's like, "No, we're gonna sit here

708
00:32:03.777 --> 00:32:05.400
"until you finish eating that."

709
00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:07.913
And so I'm like crying, eating this lobster tail.

710
00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:13.670
Cut to 20 years later,

711
00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:17.073
like I'm gambling millions of dollars in Las Vegas.

712
00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.120
Having lobsters sent up to the room,

713
00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:23.020
I have prostitutes in the room

714
00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:25.790
and I don't even eat the lobsters.

715
00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:28.960
I just check into every penthouse suite in Vegas.

716
00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:32.900
First thing I do when I went to check in is two lobsters.

717
00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:35.277
And they're like, "Oh, you gonna need it now?

718
00:32:35.277 --> 00:32:36.458
"Or do you want us to..."

719
00:32:36.458 --> 00:32:37.580
I'm like, "Just send them up to the room."

720
00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:40.380
So, there's rotting lobsters in every penthouse in Vegas.

721
00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:41.213
And I don't even eat it.

722
00:32:41.213 --> 00:32:44.133
It's just like a fuck you to my dad.

723
00:32:45.860 --> 00:32:48.660
And I didn't even put that together until I was in therapy.

724
00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:52.400
And part of the problem is what did I just tell you about,

725
00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:57.060
pugs and lobster.

726
00:32:57.060 --> 00:33:01.210
And I get into treatment and there's people there

727
00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:04.010
that have been seeing their family murdered

728
00:33:04.010 --> 00:33:04.913
in front of them.

729
00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:08.340
So then I make it a competition.

730
00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:10.350
This guy just told me his family was murdered,

731
00:33:10.350 --> 00:33:12.240
like in front of him in Cambodia.

732
00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:13.900
And now he's here and I'm gonna bring up a story

733
00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:17.020
about ordering too much lobster at dinner?

734
00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:20.700
So, I don't speak up for myself. So, I suppress it.

735
00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:24.230
I push it down. I go, dude, big deal.

736
00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:27.430
I say big deal but then it manifests in like these,

737
00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:30.820
it comes out sideways in all these strange ways.

738
00:33:30.820 --> 00:33:35.720
And do I have permission?

739
00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:36.553
Yeah.

740
00:33:36.553 --> 00:33:37.670
That what it comes down to.

741
00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:42.670
Do I have permission to forgive myself?

742
00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:44.470
Do I have permission to forgive my dad?

743
00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.600
Do I have permission to eat lobster now

744
00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:49.640
without all this guilt and shame?

745
00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:51.130
Do I have...

746
00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:55.660
And the answer in most cases of the people

747
00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:58.380
that I've been talking to is no.

748
00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.900
So, it comes out in workaholism.

749
00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:04.150
It comes out in like basically saying,

750
00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:07.880
I am not allowed to ask for permission

751
00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:12.880
for what my needs are, because I'll be rejected,

752
00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:14.460
I'll get yelled at.

753
00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.310
And so, it's like living with this ghost of...

754
00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:20.140
And if you met my dad today, he's like the sweetest guy.

755
00:34:20.140 --> 00:34:24.723
So, in the same way I go,

756
00:34:26.580 --> 00:34:29.140
he was an immigrant and he had three kids

757
00:34:29.140 --> 00:34:30.840
and he didn't have any money.

758
00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:33.790
So, I make the excuse for him,

759
00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:35.240
instead of just being like...

760
00:34:35.240 --> 00:34:37.862
But the trauma is what it is. And it doesn't matter.

761
00:34:37.862 --> 00:34:41.350
The motivations behind your parents,

762
00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:42.820
all our parents on some level,

763
00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:44.130
were doing the best that they could

764
00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:45.530
with a deck that they were dealt.

765
00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:49.550
It's that GABA Montay thing of emotional trauma,

766
00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:50.500
childhood trauma,

767
00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:53.350
like laying the foundation for these errant behaviors

768
00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:55.250
and the addictions that follow.

769
00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:59.420
We can always trace it back to something that in most cases,

770
00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:01.200
for a lot of people, it's like their parents...

771
00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.080
I'm sure your dad doesn't even remember that.

772
00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:04.159
Oh, yeah.

773
00:35:04.159 --> 00:35:05.600
It's like it was a meaningless thing in the moment.

774
00:35:05.600 --> 00:35:07.680
And yet it gets locked in, in your psyche

775
00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:11.960
and becomes this latent like pernicious seed

776
00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:13.990
that germinates over time

777
00:35:13.990 --> 00:35:16.793
and manifests in all kinds of craziness later on.

778
00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:18.643
We all have some version of that

779
00:35:18.643 --> 00:35:21.110
and that guilt and shame that we feel

780
00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:25.500
because our pain isn't as grandiose as somebody else's,

781
00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:27.297
our pain is our pain, man.

